    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "> My current update outputs the following\r\n> \r\n> ```\r\n> In [5]: from numba import cuda\r\n> \r\n> In [6]: cuda.detect()\r\n> Found 1 CUDA devices\r\n> id 0    b'GeForce GTX 1660 Ti'                              [SUPPORTED]\r\n>                       compute capability: 7.5\r\n>                            pci device id: 0\r\n>                               pci bus id: 1\r\n>                         Watchdog Enabled: 1\r\n>                             Compute Mode: 0\r\n> Single To Double Precision Performance ratio: 32\r\n> Summary:\r\n>         1/1 devices are supported\r\n> ```\r\n\r\nThis is looking nice! For \"Compute Mode\", could you translate 0 / 1 to the appropriate string of either \"WDDM\" or \"TCC\"?\r\n\r\n> I understand that the original request is to print Host to Device, Device to Host, Host to Host, FP32 and FP64 performance.\r\n> (Please correct me if I am wrong) But, These values are dynamic(based on how busy is the GPU) and we can try to get an average value by copying data multiple times and timing it.\r\n\r\nI think for now we should just leave out the performance benchmarks, and I would suggest making a PR with what you have already.\r\n\r\n> Also, I am currently printing `Single To Double Precision Performance ratio` which might be too long. Please suggest a shorter one. I am thinking of \"FP32 : FP64 performance\". But, even I don't like it.\r\n\r\nYour suggestion of \"FP32 : FP64 performance\" looks good to me.\r\n\r\n> Regarding `WDDM TDR Delay` and `WDDM TDR Enabled`, I am not sure how to get them. Based on what i read so far, this is specific to windows and it's in registry - [Link](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/display/tdr-registry-keys)\r\n\r\nI would suggest that knowing there is a watchdog enabled and being able to see whether the mode is WDDM or TCC is a good start - if the TDR delay is important, it could be added in a subsequent PR.",
